{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Bv59PRKCoyNrvHq7NJPDMp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RuCrXrZJCK1KRRGZJ58b6t","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy×Phase Interaction as an Emergent Property of Joint Compression in Reasoning LLMs\"\n\nThis paper studies the interaction between attention head pruning and KV cache eviction (compound sparsity) in reasoning LLMs. Key claims:\n1. Compound sparsity severity is an emergent property of strategy×phase interaction (not a fixed constant)\n2. Importance-based eviction shows monotonic phase gradient (Spearman ρ=1.0 across 6 model×benchmark combos)\n3. Random eviction shows negligible phase dependence (dz=0.15-0.22, none Bonferroni-significant at n=250)\n4. Discrimination collapse (Gini decline) is the mechanism (cross-model Pearson r=0.85, p=0.004)\n5. Cross-architecture generalization: LLaMA n=250 achieves dz=0.52 at s=0.30 (medium effect)\n6. Strategy specificity ratios 6.2-36× depending on task complexity\n7. Language switching as behavioral dose-response evidence\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages)\n- §1 Introduction: problem statement, 4 key findings\n- §2 Related Work: SparK, ThinKV, R-KV, Lethe, HIES, Physics of KV Cache; positions paper as first 3D (compound×phase×strategy) analysis\n- §3 Method: factorial design, 3 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B), 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond), 4 sparsity levels (0.15-0.30), 2 eviction strategies (importance, random), phase defined by normalized token position\n- §4 Experiments:\n  - §4.1 GPQA cross-benchmark phase gradient (Tables 1-2): DS gradient 0.185→4.105, QW gradient 0.042→2.350, all ρ=1.0\n  - §4.2 Strategy specificity (Tables 3-4): GPQA ratios 6.2-8.6×, cross-benchmark MATH>GPQA>GSM8K\n  - §4.3 LLaMA n=250 validation (Tables 5-6): dz improvement from n=50, 3/4 levels Bonferroni-sig, random gradient attenuation\n  - §4.4 Discrimination collapse mechanism (Tables 7-8): within-model ρ=1.0, cross-model r=0.85\n  - §4.5 Language switching (Table 9): 0%→40% monotonic dose-response\n- §5 Discussion: mechanism interpretation, task complexity modulation, architecture modulation, implications, limitations\n- §6 Conclusion\n- References: 9 citations\n\n## Experimental Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes 7 experiments. The paper covers all of them:\n1. LLaMA n=250 validation ✓ (§4.3)\n2. GPQA DeepSeek importance profiling ✓ (§4.1)\n3. GPQA Qwen importance profiling ✓ (§4.1)\n4. GPQA random baselines + strategy specificity ✓ (§4.2)\n5. Discrimination collapse Gini-II correlation ✓ (§4.4)\n6. Language switching intermediate sparsities ✓ (§4.5)\n7. Related work differentiation ✓ (§2)\n\nCoverage: 7/7 experiments covered.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on **Methods and Experiments** dimensions. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue: top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Is the factorial experimental design appropriate?\n- Is the Interaction Index (II) well-defined? Is \"degradation beyond additive prediction\" sufficiently specified?\n- Is the phase definition (normalized token position, low vs high sparsity) principled or ad-hoc?\n- Are the statistical methods appropriate (Wilcoxon, bootstrap, Bonferroni, Cohen's dz)?\n- Are there missing controls or confounds?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Sample sizes: n=50 for GPQA, n=250 for LLaMA, n=500 for mechanism. Are these adequate?\n- Is the sparsity range (0.15-0.30) well-motivated? Why not higher?\n- Are the 3 models sufficiently diverse? (2 are Qwen2 architecture)\n- Is the random baseline a fair control?\n- Reproducibility: are implementation details sufficient?\n\n### 3. Statistical Claims (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Spearman ρ=1.0 with only 4 data points—how meaningful is this?\n- Cross-model Pearson r=0.85 with only 8 data points—power concerns?\n- Bonferroni correction with k=4—appropriate?\n- Are confidence intervals adequately reported?\n- Any p-hacking or garden-of-forking-paths concerns?\n\n### 4. Completeness (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Are all claimed experiments actually present and adequately reported?\n- Missing ablations?\n- Missing baselines (e.g., other eviction strategies beyond importance and random)?\n- Should there be downstream task accuracy analysis beyond language switching?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses (bulleted list, specific and actionable)\n\n### 6. Strengths (bulleted list)\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n\n### 8. Overall Methods+Experiments Score (1-6) with 1-sentence summary\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29987,"cache_read_input_tokens":22950,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29987},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"813b3e7b-de85-4f91-a594-365a71c45c1e","uuid":"fe2775ed-1105-4cb3-81dc-426c313faab1"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a7e94aeb4a342be5d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RuCrXrZJCK1KRRGZJ58b6t","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy×Phase Interaction as an Emergent Property of Joint Compression in Reasoning LLMs\"\n\nThis paper studies the interaction between attention head pruning and KV cache eviction (compound sparsity) in reasoning LLMs. Key claims:\n1. Compound sparsity severity is an emergent property of strategy×phase interaction (not a fixed constant)\n2. Importance-based eviction shows monotonic phase gradient (Spearman ρ=1.0 across 6 model×benchmark combos)\n3. Random eviction shows negligible phase dependence (dz=0.15-0.22, none Bonferroni-significant at n=250)\n4. Discrimination collapse (Gini decline) is the mechanism (cross-model Pearson r=0.85, p=0.004)\n5. Cross-architecture generalization: LLaMA n=250 achieves dz=0.52 at s=0.30 (medium effect)\n6. Strategy specificity ratios 6.2-36× depending on task complexity\n7. Language switching as behavioral dose-response evidence\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages)\n- §1 Introduction: problem statement, 4 key findings\n- §2 Related Work: SparK, ThinKV, R-KV, Lethe, HIES, Physics of KV Cache; positions paper as first 3D (compound×phase×strategy) analysis\n- §3 Method: factorial design, 3 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B), 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond), 4 sparsity levels (0.15-0.30), 2 eviction strategies (importance, random), phase defined by normalized token position\n- §4 Experiments:\n  - §4.1 GPQA cross-benchmark phase gradient (Tables 1-2): DS gradient 0.185→4.105, QW gradient 0.042→2.350, all ρ=1.0\n  - §4.2 Strategy specificity (Tables 3-4): GPQA ratios 6.2-8.6×, cross-benchmark MATH>GPQA>GSM8K\n  - §4.3 LLaMA n=250 validation (Tables 5-6): dz improvement from n=50, 3/4 levels Bonferroni-sig, random gradient attenuation\n  - §4.4 Discrimination collapse mechanism (Tables 7-8): within-model ρ=1.0, cross-model r=0.85\n  - §4.5 Language switching (Table 9): 0%→40% monotonic dose-response\n- §5 Discussion: mechanism interpretation, task complexity modulation, architecture modulation, implications, limitations\n- §6 Conclusion\n- References: 9 citations\n\n## Experimental Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes 7 experiments. The paper covers all of them:\n1. LLaMA n=250 validation ✓ (§4.3)\n2. GPQA DeepSeek importance profiling ✓ (§4.1)\n3. GPQA Qwen importance profiling ✓ (§4.1)\n4. GPQA random baselines + strategy specificity ✓ (§4.2)\n5. Discrimination collapse Gini-II correlation ✓ (§4.4)\n6. Language switching intermediate sparsities ✓ (§4.5)\n7. Related work differentiation ✓ (§2)\n\nCoverage: 7/7 experiments covered.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on **Methods and Experiments** dimensions. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue: top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Is the factorial experimental design appropriate?\n- Is the Interaction Index (II) well-defined? Is \"degradation beyond additive prediction\" sufficiently specified?\n- Is the phase definition (normalized token position, low vs high sparsity) principled or ad-hoc?\n- Are the statistical methods appropriate (Wilcoxon, bootstrap, Bonferroni, Cohen's dz)?\n- Are there missing controls or confounds?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Sample sizes: n=50 for GPQA, n=250 for LLaMA, n=500 for mechanism. Are these adequate?\n- Is the sparsity range (0.15-0.30) well-motivated? Why not higher?\n- Are the 3 models sufficiently diverse? (2 are Qwen2 architecture)\n- Is the random baseline a fair control?\n- Reproducibility: are implementation details sufficient?\n\n### 3. Statistical Claims (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Spearman ρ=1.0 with only 4 data points—how meaningful is this?\n- Cross-model Pearson r=0.85 with only 8 data points—power concerns?\n- Bonferroni correction with k=4—appropriate?\n- Are confidence intervals adequately reported?\n- Any p-hacking or garden-of-forking-paths concerns?\n\n### 4. Completeness (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Are all claimed experiments actually present and adequately reported?\n- Missing ablations?\n- Missing baselines (e.g., other eviction strategies beyond importance and random)?\n- Should there be downstream task accuracy analysis beyond language switching?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses (bulleted list, specific and actionable)\n\n### 6. Strengths (bulleted list)\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n\n### 8. Overall Methods+Experiments Score (1-6) with 1-sentence summary\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair.","uuid":"d6e5a28b-46a1-4a43-9a2f-88a4161082de","session_id":"813b3e7b-de85-4f91-a594-365a71c45c1e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy×Phase Interaction as an Emergent Property of Joint Compression in Reasoning LLMs\"\n\nThis paper studies the interaction between attention head pruning and KV cache eviction (compound sparsity) in reasoning LLMs. Key claims:\n1. Compound sparsity severity is an emergent property of strategy×phase interaction (not a fixed constant)\n2. Importance-based eviction shows monotonic phase gradient (Spearman ρ=1.0 across 6 model×benchmark combos)\n3. Random eviction shows negligible phase dependence (dz=0.15-0.22, none Bonferroni-significant at n=250)\n4. Discrimination collapse (Gini decline) is the mechanism (cross-model Pearson r=0.85, p=0.004)\n5. Cross-architecture generalization: LLaMA n=250 achieves dz=0.52 at s=0.30 (medium effect)\n6. Strategy specificity ratios 6.2-36× depending on task complexity\n7. Language switching as behavioral dose-response evidence\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages)\n- §1 Introduction: problem statement, 4 key findings\n- §2 Related Work: SparK, ThinKV, R-KV, Lethe, HIES, Physics of KV Cache; positions paper as first 3D (compound×phase×strategy) analysis\n- §3 Method: factorial design, 3 models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B), 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond), 4 sparsity levels (0.15-0.30), 2 eviction strategies (importance, random), phase defined by normalized token position\n- §4 Experiments:\n  - §4.1 GPQA cross-benchmark phase gradient (Tables 1-2): DS gradient 0.185→4.105, QW gradient 0.042→2.350, all ρ=1.0\n  - §4.2 Strategy specificity (Tables 3-4): GPQA ratios 6.2-8.6×, cross-benchmark MATH>GPQA>GSM8K\n  - §4.3 LLaMA n=250 validation (Tables 5-6): dz improvement from n=50, 3/4 levels Bonferroni-sig, random gradient attenuation\n  - §4.4 Discrimination collapse mechanism (Tables 7-8): within-model ρ=1.0, cross-model r=0.85\n  - §4.5 Language switching (Table 9): 0%→40% monotonic dose-response\n- §5 Discussion: mechanism interpretation, task complexity modulation, architecture modulation, implications, limitations\n- §6 Conclusion\n- References: 9 citations\n\n## Experimental Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes 7 experiments. The paper covers all of them:\n1. LLaMA n=250 validation ✓ (§4.3)\n2. GPQA DeepSeek importance profiling ✓ (§4.1)\n3. GPQA Qwen importance profiling ✓ (§4.1)\n4. GPQA random baselines + strategy specificity ✓ (§4.2)\n5. Discrimination collapse Gini-II correlation ✓ (§4.4)\n6. Language switching intermediate sparsities ✓ (§4.5)\n7. Related work differentiation ✓ (§2)\n\nCoverage: 7/7 experiments covered.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on **Methods and Experiments** dimensions. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue: top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Is the factorial experimental design appropriate?\n- Is the Interaction Index (II) well-defined? Is \"degradation beyond additive prediction\" sufficiently specified?\n- Is the phase definition (normalized token position, low vs high sparsity) principled or ad-hoc?\n- Are the statistical methods appropriate (Wilcoxon, bootstrap, Bonferroni, Cohen's dz)?\n- Are there missing controls or confounds?\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Sample sizes: n=50 for GPQA, n=250 for LLaMA, n=500 for mechanism. Are these adequate?\n- Is the sparsity range (0.15-0.30) well-motivated? Why not higher?\n- Are the 3 models sufficiently diverse? (2 are Qwen2 architecture)\n- Is the random baseline a fair control?\n- Reproducibility: are implementation details sufficient?\n\n### 3. Statistical Claims (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Spearman ρ=1.0 with only 4 data points—how meaningful is this?\n- Cross-model Pearson r=0.85 with only 8 data points—power concerns?\n- Bonferroni correction with k=4—appropriate?\n- Are confidence intervals adequately reported?\n- Any p-hacking or garden-of-forking-paths concerns?\n\n### 4. Completeness (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Are all claimed experiments actually present and adequately reported?\n- Missing ablations?\n- Missing baselines (e.g., other eviction strategies beyond importance and random)?\n- Should there be downstream task accuracy analysis beyond language switching?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses (bulleted list, specific and actionable)\n\n### 6. Strengths (bulleted list)\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n\n### 8. Overall Methods+Experiments Score (1-6) with 1-sentence summary\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RuCrXrZJCK1KRRGZJ58b6t","session_id":"813b3e7b-de85-4f91-a594-365a71c45c1e","uuid":"5960a19c-9f1b-48c4-a6eb-911319866278","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:06:02.230Z"}
