{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RTqJUQbb2RCtXuAZrX68A8","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment — Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Core finding (strategy x phase interaction as emergent property).**\nThe central observation — that compound sparsity severity varies across reasoning phases and eviction strategies rather than being a fixed constant — is a reasonable empirical contribution, but I have reservations about whether it rises to the novelty bar of a top venue.\n\nSeveral prior works already establish the constituent pieces individually: ThinKV (ICLR 2026) demonstrates phase-dependent compression tolerance; HIES (2025) documents importance score collapse under pruning; SparK (AAAI 2026) documents super-additive compound degradation; the \"Physics of KV Cache\" paper (2026) identifies sharp phase transitions in compression; and DefensiveKV (ICLR 2026) independently discovers importance-based eviction fragility. The paper's contribution is to compose these known phenomena — phase dependence, score collapse, and compound interaction — and measure their joint behavior. This is a valid scientific question, but the answer (\"yes, they interact, and the interaction is phase-dependent\") is arguably the expected outcome given the priors. The paper itself acknowledges that ThinKV's phase-aware perspective and HIES's score collapse are independently known; the novelty claim rests on their composition.\n\n**\"Discrimination collapse\" as a new insight.**\nThis is largely a repackaging of HIES's importance score collapse, extended to the compound setting. The paper is commendably transparent about this (Section 6 states \"HIES report this collapse under single-axis pruning; we extend it to the compound setting\"), but the extension is incremental. The per-phase Gini analysis adds granularity, but the core phenomenon — importance scores become less discriminative under pruning — was known. The paper's own analysis acknowledges the mechanism is correlational and that \"aggregate concentration collapse alone is insufficient\" to explain the 15-17x severity divergence, which weakens its explanatory power.\n\n**\"Compound x phase x strategy\" 3D framing.**\nThe framing is well-articulated, but the actual experimental design is a 2x4x2 factorial on perplexity. The \"3D\" framing makes this sound more multidimensional than it is — it is a systematic measurement study across known axes of variation. The \"framework\" contribution (phase-gated analysis) is essentially computing an existing metric (II) per phase segment, which is operationally straightforward.\n\n**\"Emergent property\" framing.**\nCalling a phase-dependent interaction an \"emergent property\" is overclaiming. Emergence implies qualitatively new behavior that cannot be predicted from constituent parts. The authors show a quantitative modulation of a known interaction (compound super-additivity) by a known variable (reasoning phase). This is a measured interaction effect, not emergence. The title uses the more restrained \"Phase-Modulated,\" which is appropriate; the abstract's use of \"emergent\" is not.\n\n**Contribution type.**\nThis is an empirical measurement paper, not a method paper. Empirical finding papers can be valuable, but the finding needs to be surprising or actionable. The finding is somewhat expected (given the prior literature) and the actionability is weak (the pilot mitigation study failed its pre-registered gate at 18% recovery vs. 30% target).\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness — Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\nThe related work has been substantially expanded from the initial summary I was given. The actual paper cites ~35 works across three well-organized paragraphs (compound compression, KV cache eviction, phase-aware inference). This is a marked improvement over the 9-reference version described in the review prompt.\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- StreamingLLM, DuoAttention, SnapKV, PyramidKV, Ada-KV, NACL, DefensiveKV are all cited in the KV cache eviction paragraph.\n- GRASPrune, SLiM, UniComp, and phase-transition-compression are cited for compound compression.\n- PM-KVQ and Kim et al. (PQQP) on compression ordering add depth.\n- ThinKV, R-KV, Physics of KV Cache, HIES are properly positioned.\n- The concurrent work citation (Hold Onto That Thought) is appropriate.\n\n**Missing or underrepresented:**\n- **FastGen** (Ge et al., 2024 — \"Model Tells You What to Discard\") is absent despite being a foundational adaptive KV compression work.\n- **Attention head importance/redundancy literature** beyond Voita (2019) and Michel (2019): the substantial body of work on attention head analysis (Clark et al. 2019 \"What Does BERT Look At?\", Kovaleva et al. 2019, etc.) is not engaged with, which is relevant to the discrimination collapse argument.\n- **Compound compression in vision/speech domains** is entirely absent. Joint pruning+quantization has a long history in vision (e.g., Han et al. 2016 \"Deep Compression\"). While the LLM-specific focus is reasonable, acknowledging this broader context would strengthen positioning.\n- **MoE-related sparsity work** is absent — reasoning models increasingly use mixture-of-experts, and the interaction between MoE routing and KV cache sparsity is a relevant related direction.\n- **SqueezeAttention** (ICLR 2025) is mentioned in the literature_map.yaml but absent from the paper — it provides a 2D (layer x sequence) budget optimization that is directly relevant as a precursor to the authors' 3D claim.\n\n**Positioning claim defensibility:**\nThe claim \"No prior work simultaneously analyzes pruning x eviction compound interaction across reasoning phases and eviction strategies\" appears defensible based on my reading. The specific combination is novel, even if the individual components are not. However, the phrasing could be tightened — PM-KVQ does introduce a temporal dimension to compression severity, and the paper itself cites it as \"implicitly introducing a temporal dimension to compression severity that resonates with our finding.\"\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact — Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Community impact.**\nThe finding that compound sparsity severity is phase-dependent is useful diagnostic knowledge, but its practical impact is unclear. The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper, not a method paper — which is fine in principle, but the finding needs to be either surprising enough to change mental models or actionable enough to change practice.\n\n**Actionability.**\nThe main practical implication — phase-aware budget allocation — was tested in a pilot study that failed its pre-registered gate (18% oracle recovery, CI spans zero). The paper honestly reports this, which is commendable, but it undermines the practical significance claim. If the finding cannot be leveraged for even modest mitigation with oracle phase boundaries, its deployment implications are aspirational rather than demonstrated.\n\n**Diagnostic vs. constructive.**\nThis is primarily a diagnostic/negative result paper: it characterizes where compound sparsity fails rather than offering a solution. Such papers have value, but typically need to either (a) identify a clear, previously unknown failure mode, or (b) provide a framework that enables new solutions. The failure mode (compound interaction amplified in low-sparsity phases) is somewhat intuitive — phases with broadly distributed attention are where importance-based methods are most fragile, because there is less signal for importance ranking. The framework (computing II per phase) is operationally simple.\n\n**Scale limitations.**\nAll experiments are on 7B models. The Qwen2.5-14B results are listed as \"pending\" (Table 5), and the LLaMA cross-architecture result is n=49 with weak statistical evidence. For a finding paper claiming to characterize a general property of compound sparsity in reasoning LLMs, this is a narrow empirical base.\n\n### 4. Framing and Claims — Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**\"Emergent property\" framing.**\nAs noted above, this is overclaiming. The abstract states \"compound sparsity severity is not a fixed constant but an emergent property of the interaction among eviction strategy, reasoning phase, and sparsity level.\" A more accurate framing: compound sparsity severity is *modulated by* reasoning phase and eviction strategy. This is an interaction effect, not emergence.\n\n**Scope vs. claims.**\nThe paper's scope (7B models, MATH-500 primary, GSM8K secondary, perplexity-based metric) is appropriate for the experiments conducted, but the claims in the abstract and introduction reach beyond this scope. Phrases like \"establish phase-gated analysis as a principled framework\" and \"direct implications for phase-aware budget allocation in inference systems\" overstate what the paper demonstrates, especially given the failed mitigation pilot.\n\n**Title appropriateness.**\nThe actual title in main.tex — \"Phase-Modulated Compound Sparsity Severity in 7B-Scale Mathematical Reasoning Models\" — is appropriately scoped. The title given in the review prompt (\"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy x Phase Interaction as an Emergent Property of Joint Compression in Reasoning LLMs\") would be overclaiming.\n\n**Placeholder sections.**\nTables 5 (Qwen2.5-14B) and 7 (GPQA Diamond) contain \"Results pending\" placeholders. Submitting a paper with empty results tables is a serious issue for a camera-ready or even an ARR submission. These sections should either be completed or removed.\n\n**Statistical claims.**\nThe statistical methodology is thorough (paired bootstrap, Wilcoxon, Bonferroni, Jonckheere-Terpstra, BH-FDR robustness check). The Spearman rho=1.0 claim on 4 data points (4 sparsity levels) is correctly caveated in the methodology as \"interpret with caution,\" and the JT test on per-problem values is the primary evidence. This is appropriately handled.\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n- **Low novelty ceiling**: The core finding is a measured interaction of individually known phenomena (phase-dependent compression tolerance + compound super-additivity + importance score collapse). The composition is systematic but predictable given the priors.\n- **\"Emergent\" framing is overclaiming**: The observed effect is a quantitative modulation, not a qualitatively new phenomenon. The paper should use \"phase-modulated\" consistently and drop \"emergent.\"\n- **Failed mitigation pilot undermines practical significance**: The pre-registered 30% gate was not met (18% recovery, CI spans zero). This leaves the \"implications for deployment\" as aspirational.\n- **Placeholder results tables**: Sections 5.5 (14B) and 5.8 (GPQA) have empty tables marked \"Results pending\" — the paper is incomplete as submitted.\n- **Narrow model scale**: Only 7B models with confirmed results. The 14B and LLaMA results are either pending or statistically weak (n=49).\n- **Mechanism is correlational and acknowledged as insufficient**: The paper states that Gini collapse \"alone is insufficient\" (both strategies show similar Gini drops yet severity diverges 15-17x). The mechanistic contribution is therefore limited.\n- **Perplexity-only primary metric**: The phase-level analysis cannot be done with accuracy (acknowledged in limitations), but this means the core finding is about perplexity degradation patterns, not about downstream task failure patterns. The accuracy validation (Section 5.10) is limited and shows a dual-regime structure that does not straightforwardly map to the PPL-based findings.\n- **Circular confound in importance-based eviction**: The paper acknowledges (Limitations) that head pruning alters the attention distribution from which importance scores are computed. The compound interaction patterns may reflect this circular dependency rather than a general property of compound sparsity. This is a fundamental interpretability limitation.\n- **Missing SqueezeAttention citation**: SqueezeAttention (ICLR 2025) is the direct 2D (layer x sequence) precursor to the authors' claimed 3D analysis but is not cited in the paper.\n\n### 6. Strengths\n\n- **Rigorous statistical methodology**: Paired bootstrap with B=10,000, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Bonferroni correction, Jonckheere-Terpstra for monotonicity, BH-FDR robustness check, and scaling from n=50 to n=500. This is well above average for the field.\n- **Honest reporting of negative results**: The failed mitigation pilot, the Qwen entropy reversal, the LLaMA weak interaction, and the GSM8K specificity attenuation are all transparently reported. The paper does not cherry-pick.\n- **Pre-registered gates**: The 30% recovery gate for C5b was pre-registered, and the paper honestly reports failure. This is rare and commendable.\n- **Comprehensive limitations section**: The paper identifies most of its own weaknesses (circular confound, perplexity-only, model scale, phase segmentation validation limited to one model, correlational mechanism).\n- **Cross-model and cross-benchmark replication**: Two models (DeepSeek, Qwen) with n=500, one benchmark replication (GSM8K), and a preliminary cross-architecture check (LLaMA). The replication discipline is good.\n- **Well-organized related work**: Three focused paragraphs with clear positioning statements and ~35 references covering the relevant landscape.\n- **Thoughtful experimental design**: The factorial structure (2 strategies x 4 sparsity levels x 2 models x 2 benchmarks) is systematic. The use of random eviction as a control condition is a good experimental design choice.\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the circular confound**: You acknowledge that importance-based eviction operates on scores degraded by the very head pruning it is composed with. Have you considered testing an eviction strategy whose scoring is independent of the pruned attention distribution (e.g., position-based or learned)? Without this control, how can you distinguish \"compound sparsity is phase-dependent\" from \"importance score degradation is phase-dependent\"?\n\n2. **On the failed mitigation**: The pilot study achieved 18% oracle recovery (CI: [-39%, 46%]). Given that even oracle phase boundaries do not yield statistically meaningful improvement, what evidence supports the claim that \"effective phase-aware allocation likely requires finer phase boundary estimation\"? Could the finding simply be that phase-aware allocation is not a viable mitigation strategy?\n\n3. **On GSM8K specificity attenuation**: Strategy specificity drops from 15-17x on MATH-500 to a qualitative gap (significant vs. non-significant) on GSM8K, and the Qwen GSM8K data is confounded by truncation. With only one clean cross-benchmark replication (DeepSeek GSM8K), how confident are you that the phase gradient generalizes beyond MATH-500?\n\n4. **On the placeholder sections**: Sections 5.5 (Qwen2.5-14B) and 5.8 (GPQA Diamond) contain empty results tables. Are these experiments expected to complete before the ARR deadline? If not, how does the paper's narrative change without them?\n\n5. **On \"emergent\" vs. \"modulated\"**: The title uses \"Phase-Modulated\" but the abstract uses \"emergent property.\" Given that the individual phenomena (phase-dependent sensitivity, compound super-additivity, importance collapse) are all known, what specifically makes their composition \"emergent\" rather than \"expected\"?\n\n### 8. Overall Novelty + Related Work Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Summary**: The paper presents a systematic empirical measurement of a three-way interaction (strategy x phase x sparsity) in compound LLM compression, supported by rigorous statistics and honest reporting. However, the core finding is a predictable composition of individually known phenomena, the proposed mechanism is correlational and self-admittedly insufficient, the practical implications are not demonstrated (failed mitigation pilot), and the paper contains incomplete sections (placeholder tables). The related work is comprehensive. The contribution is below the novelty threshold for a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate, though it would be a solid workshop paper or a contribution to a venue emphasizing empirical methodology."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac8a00f1a7fa967b7 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac8a00f1a7fa967b7' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 76017\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 151227</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"813b3e7b-de85-4f91-a594-365a71c45c1e","uuid":"34a5c9a5-ae19-4889-8245-e19df06488ef","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:08:55.440Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy×Phase Interaction as an Emergent Property of Joint Compression in Reasoning LLMs\"\n\nThis paper studies the interaction between attention head pruning and KV cache eviction (compound sparsity) in reasoning LLMs. Key claims:\n1. Compound sparsity severity is an emergent property of strategy×phase interaction (not a fixed constant)\n2. Importance-based eviction shows monotonic phase gradient (Spearman ρ=1.0 across 6 model×benchmark combos)\n3. Random eviction shows negligible phase dependence\n4. Discrimination collapse (Gini decline) is the mechanism (cross-model Pearson r=0.85)\n5. Cross-architecture generalization confirmed on LLaMA (dz=0.52)\n6. Strategy specificity ratios 6.2-36×\n7. Language switching as behavioral evidence\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages)\n- §1 Introduction: 4 key findings\n- §2 Related Work: 6 paragraphs covering SparK (compound compression, uniform), ThinKV (phase-aware single-axis), R-KV (task-specific single-axis), Lethe (adaptive eviction method), HIES (importance score collapse single-axis), Physics of KV Cache (compression-rate phase transitions)\n  - Positioning statement: \"No prior work simultaneously analyzes pruning×eviction compound interaction across reasoning phases and eviction strategies.\"\n- §3 Method: factorial design, 3 models, 3 benchmarks, 4 sparsity, 2 strategies\n  - Models: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (DS), Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (QW), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LLaMA)\n  - Benchmarks: MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA-Diamond\n  - Sparsity: s ∈ {0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30}\n  - Strategies: importance-based (accumulated attention scores), random\n  - Metrics: Interaction Index (II), Phase Gradient, Cohen's dz\n- §4 Experiments: 5 subsections (GPQA cross-benchmark, strategy specificity, LLaMA validation, discrimination collapse, language switching)\n- §5 Discussion: mechanism, task complexity modulation, architecture modulation, implications (phase-aware allocation), pilot study mention (18% recovery vs 30% target), limitations (7B only, task-dependent magnitude, empirical not theoretical)\n- §6 Conclusion\n- References: 9 total (Zhang 2024 H2O, Liu 2024 Scissorhands, Ge 2024 Model tells you, Chen 2024 SparK, Xu 2025 ThinKV, Wang 2025 R-KV, Li 2026 Lethe, Zhou 2025 HIES, Tan 2026 Physics of KV)\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on **Novelty and Related Work** dimensions. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue: top ML venue with ~25% acceptance rate.\n\nPlease provide:\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Is the core finding (strategy×phase interaction as emergent property) genuinely novel?\n- Is \"discrimination collapse\" a new insight or a repackaging of known phenomena (e.g., HIES's importance score collapse)?\n- Is the \"compound × phase × strategy\" 3D framing a substantive contribution or an incremental extension?\n- Does the paper advance understanding beyond what practitioners already know intuitively?\n- Is this an empirical finding paper or a method paper? Is the contribution level appropriate for a top venue?\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Are the 6 related work categories comprehensive?\n- Missing important references? Consider:\n  - Other KV cache compression methods (SnapKV, PyramidKV, StreamingLLM, FastGen, etc.)\n  - Structured pruning literature beyond the cited works\n  - Compound/joint compression in other domains (vision, speech)\n  - Reasoning-specific compression work\n  - Attention head importance/redundancy studies\n- Is the positioning (\"no prior work simultaneously analyzes...\") defensible?\n- Are the distinctions from prior work genuine or straw-man?\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact (score 1-6 + justification)\n- How impactful is this finding for the community?\n- Does it change how practitioners design compression systems?\n- Is the implication (phase-aware allocation) actionable? The pilot study showed only 18% recovery...\n- Is this primarily a negative/diagnostic result or does it offer constructive guidance?\n\n### 4. Framing and Claims (score 1-6 + justification)\n- Is \"emergent property\" the right framing or overclaiming?\n- Is the paper appropriately scoped for its contribution level?\n- Are the claims in the abstract/intro well-supported by the experiments?\n- Is the title appropriate?\n\n### 5. Weaknesses (bulleted list, specific and actionable)\n\n### 6. Strengths (bulleted list)\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n\n### 8. Overall Novelty+Related Work Score (1-6) with 1-sentence summary\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair.","agentId":"ac8a00f1a7fa967b7","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment — Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Core finding (strategy x phase interaction as emergent property).**\nThe central observation — that compound sparsity severity varies across reasoning phases and eviction strategies rather than being a fixed constant — is a reasonable empirical contribution, but I have reservations about whether it rises to the novelty bar of a top venue.\n\nSeveral prior works already establish the constituent pieces individually: ThinKV (ICLR 2026) demonstrates phase-dependent compression tolerance; HIES (2025) documents importance score collapse under pruning; SparK (AAAI 2026) documents super-additive compound degradation; the \"Physics of KV Cache\" paper (2026) identifies sharp phase transitions in compression; and DefensiveKV (ICLR 2026) independently discovers importance-based eviction fragility. The paper's contribution is to compose these known phenomena — phase dependence, score collapse, and compound interaction — and measure their joint behavior. This is a valid scientific question, but the answer (\"yes, they interact, and the interaction is phase-dependent\") is arguably the expected outcome given the priors. The paper itself acknowledges that ThinKV's phase-aware perspective and HIES's score collapse are independently known; the novelty claim rests on their composition.\n\n**\"Discrimination collapse\" as a new insight.**\nThis is largely a repackaging of HIES's importance score collapse, extended to the compound setting. The paper is commendably transparent about this (Section 6 states \"HIES report this collapse under single-axis pruning; we extend it to the compound setting\"), but the extension is incremental. The per-phase Gini analysis adds granularity, but the core phenomenon — importance scores become less discriminative under pruning — was known. The paper's own analysis acknowledges the mechanism is correlational and that \"aggregate concentration collapse alone is insufficient\" to explain the 15-17x severity divergence, which weakens its explanatory power.\n\n**\"Compound x phase x strategy\" 3D framing.**\nThe framing is well-articulated, but the actual experimental design is a 2x4x2 factorial on perplexity. The \"3D\" framing makes this sound more multidimensional than it is — it is a systematic measurement study across known axes of variation. The \"framework\" contribution (phase-gated analysis) is essentially computing an existing metric (II) per phase segment, which is operationally straightforward.\n\n**\"Emergent property\" framing.**\nCalling a phase-dependent interaction an \"emergent property\" is overclaiming. Emergence implies qualitatively new behavior that cannot be predicted from constituent parts. The authors show a quantitative modulation of a known interaction (compound super-additivity) by a known variable (reasoning phase). This is a measured interaction effect, not emergence. The title uses the more restrained \"Phase-Modulated,\" which is appropriate; the abstract's use of \"emergent\" is not.\n\n**Contribution type.**\nThis is an empirical measurement paper, not a method paper. Empirical finding papers can be valuable, but the finding needs to be surprising or actionable. The finding is somewhat expected (given the prior literature) and the actionability is weak (the pilot mitigation study failed its pre-registered gate at 18% recovery vs. 30% target).\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness — Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\nThe related work has been substantially expanded from the initial summary I was given. The actual paper cites ~35 works across three well-organized paragraphs (compound compression, KV cache eviction, phase-aware inference). This is a marked improvement over the 9-reference version described in the review prompt.\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- StreamingLLM, DuoAttention, SnapKV, PyramidKV, Ada-KV, NACL, DefensiveKV are all cited in the KV cache eviction paragraph.\n- GRASPrune, SLiM, UniComp, and phase-transition-compression are cited for compound compression.\n- PM-KVQ and Kim et al. (PQQP) on compression ordering add depth.\n- ThinKV, R-KV, Physics of KV Cache, HIES are properly positioned.\n- The concurrent work citation (Hold Onto That Thought) is appropriate.\n\n**Missing or underrepresented:**\n- **FastGen** (Ge et al., 2024 — \"Model Tells You What to Discard\") is absent despite being a foundational adaptive KV compression work.\n- **Attention head importance/redundancy literature** beyond Voita (2019) and Michel (2019): the substantial body of work on attention head analysis (Clark et al. 2019 \"What Does BERT Look At?\", Kovaleva et al. 2019, etc.) is not engaged with, which is relevant to the discrimination collapse argument.\n- **Compound compression in vision/speech domains** is entirely absent. Joint pruning+quantization has a long history in vision (e.g., Han et al. 2016 \"Deep Compression\"). While the LLM-specific focus is reasonable, acknowledging this broader context would strengthen positioning.\n- **MoE-related sparsity work** is absent — reasoning models increasingly use mixture-of-experts, and the interaction between MoE routing and KV cache sparsity is a relevant related direction.\n- **SqueezeAttention** (ICLR 2025) is mentioned in the literature_map.yaml but absent from the paper — it provides a 2D (layer x sequence) budget optimization that is directly relevant as a precursor to the authors' 3D claim.\n\n**Positioning claim defensibility:**\nThe claim \"No prior work simultaneously analyzes pruning x eviction compound interaction across reasoning phases and eviction strategies\" appears defensible based on my reading. The specific combination is novel, even if the individual components are not. However, the phrasing could be tightened — PM-KVQ does introduce a temporal dimension to compression severity, and the paper itself cites it as \"implicitly introducing a temporal dimension to compression severity that resonates with our finding.\"\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact — Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Community impact.**\nThe finding that compound sparsity severity is phase-dependent is useful diagnostic knowledge, but its practical impact is unclear. The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper, not a method paper — which is fine in principle, but the finding needs to be either surprising enough to change mental models or actionable enough to change practice.\n\n**Actionability.**\nThe main practical implication — phase-aware budget allocation — was tested in a pilot study that failed its pre-registered gate (18% oracle recovery, CI spans zero). The paper honestly reports this, which is commendable, but it undermines the practical significance claim. If the finding cannot be leveraged for even modest mitigation with oracle phase boundaries, its deployment implications are aspirational rather than demonstrated.\n\n**Diagnostic vs. constructive.**\nThis is primarily a diagnostic/negative result paper: it characterizes where compound sparsity fails rather than offering a solution. Such papers have value, but typically need to either (a) identify a clear, previously unknown failure mode, or (b) provide a framework that enables new solutions. The failure mode (compound interaction amplified in low-sparsity phases) is somewhat intuitive — phases with broadly distributed attention are where importance-based methods are most fragile, because there is less signal for importance ranking. The framework (computing II per phase) is operationally simple.\n\n**Scale limitations.**\nAll experiments are on 7B models. The Qwen2.5-14B results are listed as \"pending\" (Table 5), and the LLaMA cross-architecture result is n=49 with weak statistical evidence. For a finding paper claiming to characterize a general property of compound sparsity in reasoning LLMs, this is a narrow empirical base.\n\n### 4. Framing and Claims — Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**\"Emergent property\" framing.**\nAs noted above, this is overclaiming. The abstract states \"compound sparsity severity is not a fixed constant but an emergent property of the interaction among eviction strategy, reasoning phase, and sparsity level.\" A more accurate framing: compound sparsity severity is *modulated by* reasoning phase and eviction strategy. This is an interaction effect, not emergence.\n\n**Scope vs. claims.**\nThe paper's scope (7B models, MATH-500 primary, GSM8K secondary, perplexity-based metric) is appropriate for the experiments conducted, but the claims in the abstract and introduction reach beyond this scope. Phrases like \"establish phase-gated analysis as a principled framework\" and \"direct implications for phase-aware budget allocation in inference systems\" overstate what the paper demonstrates, especially given the failed mitigation pilot.\n\n**Title appropriateness.**\nThe actual title in main.tex — \"Phase-Modulated Compound Sparsity Severity in 7B-Scale Mathematical Reasoning Models\" — is appropriately scoped. The title given in the review prompt (\"Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity: Strategy x Phase Interaction as an Emergent Property of Joint Compression in Reasoning LLMs\") would be overclaiming.\n\n**Placeholder sections.**\nTables 5 (Qwen2.5-14B) and 7 (GPQA Diamond) contain \"Results pending\" placeholders. Submitting a paper with empty results tables is a serious issue for a camera-ready or even an ARR submission. These sections should either be completed or removed.\n\n**Statistical claims.**\nThe statistical methodology is thorough (paired bootstrap, Wilcoxon, Bonferroni, Jonckheere-Terpstra, BH-FDR robustness check). The Spearman rho=1.0 claim on 4 data points (4 sparsity levels) is correctly caveated in the methodology as \"interpret with caution,\" and the JT test on per-problem values is the primary evidence. This is appropriately handled.\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n- **Low novelty ceiling**: The core finding is a measured interaction of individually known phenomena (phase-dependent compression tolerance + compound super-additivity + importance score collapse). The composition is systematic but predictable given the priors.\n- **\"Emergent\" framing is overclaiming**: The observed effect is a quantitative modulation, not a qualitatively new phenomenon. The paper should use \"phase-modulated\" consistently and drop \"emergent.\"\n- **Failed mitigation pilot undermines practical significance**: The pre-registered 30% gate was not met (18% recovery, CI spans zero). This leaves the \"implications for deployment\" as aspirational.\n- **Placeholder results tables**: Sections 5.5 (14B) and 5.8 (GPQA) have empty tables marked \"Results pending\" — the paper is incomplete as submitted.\n- **Narrow model scale**: Only 7B models with confirmed results. The 14B and LLaMA results are either pending or statistically weak (n=49).\n- **Mechanism is correlational and acknowledged as insufficient**: The paper states that Gini collapse \"alone is insufficient\" (both strategies show similar Gini drops yet severity diverges 15-17x). The mechanistic contribution is therefore limited.\n- **Perplexity-only primary metric**: The phase-level analysis cannot be done with accuracy (acknowledged in limitations), but this means the core finding is about perplexity degradation patterns, not about downstream task failure patterns. The accuracy validation (Section 5.10) is limited and shows a dual-regime structure that does not straightforwardly map to the PPL-based findings.\n- **Circular confound in importance-based eviction**: The paper acknowledges (Limitations) that head pruning alters the attention distribution from which importance scores are computed. The compound interaction patterns may reflect this circular dependency rather than a general property of compound sparsity. This is a fundamental interpretability limitation.\n- **Missing SqueezeAttention citation**: SqueezeAttention (ICLR 2025) is the direct 2D (layer x sequence) precursor to the authors' claimed 3D analysis but is not cited in the paper.\n\n### 6. Strengths\n\n- **Rigorous statistical methodology**: Paired bootstrap with B=10,000, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Bonferroni correction, Jonckheere-Terpstra for monotonicity, BH-FDR robustness check, and scaling from n=50 to n=500. This is well above average for the field.\n- **Honest reporting of negative results**: The failed mitigation pilot, the Qwen entropy reversal, the LLaMA weak interaction, and the GSM8K specificity attenuation are all transparently reported. The paper does not cherry-pick.\n- **Pre-registered gates**: The 30% recovery gate for C5b was pre-registered, and the paper honestly reports failure. This is rare and commendable.\n- **Comprehensive limitations section**: The paper identifies most of its own weaknesses (circular confound, perplexity-only, model scale, phase segmentation validation limited to one model, correlational mechanism).\n- **Cross-model and cross-benchmark replication**: Two models (DeepSeek, Qwen) with n=500, one benchmark replication (GSM8K), and a preliminary cross-architecture check (LLaMA). The replication discipline is good.\n- **Well-organized related work**: Three focused paragraphs with clear positioning statements and ~35 references covering the relevant landscape.\n- **Thoughtful experimental design**: The factorial structure (2 strategies x 4 sparsity levels x 2 models x 2 benchmarks) is systematic. The use of random eviction as a control condition is a good experimental design choice.\n\n### 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the circular confound**: You acknowledge that importance-based eviction operates on scores degraded by the very head pruning it is composed with. Have you considered testing an eviction strategy whose scoring is independent of the pruned attention distribution (e.g., position-based or learned)? Without this control, how can you distinguish \"compound sparsity is phase-dependent\" from \"importance score degradation is phase-dependent\"?\n\n2. **On the failed mitigation**: The pilot study achieved 18% oracle recovery (CI: [-39%, 46%]). Given that even oracle phase boundaries do not yield statistically meaningful improvement, what evidence supports the claim that \"effective phase-aware allocation likely requires finer phase boundary estimation\"? Could the finding simply be that phase-aware allocation is not a viable mitigation strategy?\n\n3. **On GSM8K specificity attenuation**: Strategy specificity drops from 15-17x on MATH-500 to a qualitative gap (significant vs. non-significant) on GSM8K, and the Qwen GSM8K data is confounded by truncation. With only one clean cross-benchmark replication (DeepSeek GSM8K), how confident are you that the phase gradient generalizes beyond MATH-500?\n\n4. **On the placeholder sections**: Sections 5.5 (Qwen2.5-14B) and 5.8 (GPQA Diamond) contain empty results tables. Are these experiments expected to complete before the ARR deadline? If not, how does the paper's narrative change without them?\n\n5. **On \"emergent\" vs. \"modulated\"**: The title uses \"Phase-Modulated\" but the abstract uses \"emergent property.\" Given that the individual phenomena (phase-dependent sensitivity, compound super-additivity, importance collapse) are all known, what specifically makes their composition \"emergent\" rather than \"expected\"?\n\n### 8. Overall Novelty + Related Work Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Summary**: The paper presents a systematic empirical measurement of a three-way interaction (strategy x phase x sparsity) in compound LLM compression, supported by rigorous statistics and honest reporting. However, the core finding is a predictable composition of individually known phenomena, the proposed mechanism is correlational and self-admittedly insufficient, the practical implications are not demonstrated (failed mitigation pilot), and the paper contains incomplete sections (placeholder tables). The related work is comprehensive. The contribution is below the novelty threshold for a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate, though it would be a solid workshop paper or a contribution to a venue emphasizing empirical methodology."}],"totalDurationMs":151227,"totalTokens":76017,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":71529,"output_tokens":3800,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3800,"cache_read_input_tokens":71529,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
